WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.248 --> 00:00:03.773
- Hello and welcome to ED
Talks with Khan Academy

00:00:03.773 --> 00:00:07.399
where we talk to influential
people in the education space

00:00:07.399 --> 00:00:09.196
about learning and teaching.

00:00:09.196 --> 00:00:13.009
Today, we are pleased to
welcome Dr. Barbara Oakley

00:00:13.009 --> 00:00:16.907
who is celebrating the
launch of her new book,

00:00:16.907 --> 00:00:19.330
"Uncommon Sense Teaching,"

00:00:19.330 --> 00:00:22.535
and we will talk more
about that coming up.

00:00:22.535 --> 00:00:23.977
Before we get started,

00:00:23.977 --> 00:00:28.332
I wanted to remind you that
Khan Academy is a nonprofit

00:00:28.332 --> 00:00:31.058
and we are able to do our work

00:00:31.058 --> 00:00:35.412
providing a free world-class
education to anyone, anywhere

00:00:35.412 --> 00:00:37.569
thanks to donations from folks like you.

00:00:37.569 --> 00:00:40.179
So, if you are in a place
where you are able to do that,

00:00:40.179 --> 00:00:44.015
you can find a link right
at the top of our website

00:00:44.015 --> 00:00:45.956
to make any kind of donation

00:00:45.956 --> 00:00:48.614
that helps us keep continuing this work.

00:00:48.614 --> 00:00:50.429
Thank you very much.

00:00:50.429 --> 00:00:53.762
In addition, over the COVID-19 pandemic,

00:00:54.988 --> 00:00:58.538
we've had some sponsors
who have really helped us

00:00:58.538 --> 00:01:01.851
to continue that learning and keep working

00:01:01.851 --> 00:01:04.581
and making sure we're able
to provide that education

00:01:04.581 --> 00:01:09.058
so we wanna in particular
thank Bank of America, AT&amp;T,

00:01:09.058 --> 00:01:13.975
Google.org, Novartis, Fastly
and General Motors, thank you.

00:01:15.415 --> 00:01:18.165
Next, if you are looking for more

00:01:19.305 --> 00:01:21.724
of these kinds of conversations,

00:01:21.724 --> 00:01:24.181
you can find them via podcast.

00:01:24.181 --> 00:01:29.174
Our Homeroom with Sal, The
Podcast features audio versions

00:01:29.174 --> 00:01:31.179
of many of these chats we have had

00:01:31.179 --> 00:01:36.179
so do check that out wherever
you get your podcasts from

00:01:36.224 --> 00:01:39.204
and as I said in the beginning,

00:01:39.204 --> 00:01:42.887
we are excited today to be
joined by Dr. Barbara Oakley

00:01:42.887 --> 00:01:45.538
whose book, "Uncommon Sense Teaching,"

00:01:45.538 --> 00:01:49.038
Practical Insights in Brain
Science to Help Students Learn

00:01:49.038 --> 00:01:50.189
is out today.

00:01:50.189 --> 00:01:51.352
We're gonna be talking a lot

00:01:51.352 --> 00:01:54.207
about how research about
the brain helps inform us

00:01:54.207 --> 00:01:56.668
about the things we should
do in our classrooms

00:01:56.668 --> 00:01:59.332
to help more students learn more.

00:01:59.332 --> 00:02:02.322
So, welcome, it's so nice to have you.

00:02:02.322 --> 00:02:05.239
- Oh, it's good to be here Kristen.

00:02:06.267 --> 00:02:08.956
- Great, so, to get things started,

00:02:08.956 --> 00:02:10.293
I often like to talk to people

00:02:10.293 --> 00:02:14.074
about how they got to
be experts in learning.

00:02:14.074 --> 00:02:17.252
I know lots of people take
different varying paths

00:02:17.252 --> 00:02:19.367
to get here, what was yours?

00:02:19.367 --> 00:02:22.700
- Oh, I guess a bit different than some.

00:02:24.735 --> 00:02:26.188
My father was a veterinarian

00:02:26.188 --> 00:02:30.099
and so I grew up thinking,
"Whoa, I love animals,

00:02:30.099 --> 00:02:32.603
I want to be a veterinarian
just like him,"

00:02:32.603 --> 00:02:36.476
except that I couldn't do math at all

00:02:36.476 --> 00:02:38.727
so, I finally realized
that I have to give up

00:02:38.727 --> 00:02:41.150
on my childhood dream,

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:45.483
probably hoped that I did
a lot of cleaning of cages

00:02:46.338 --> 00:02:47.171
when I was a kid

00:02:47.171 --> 00:02:48.920
and that can really change your mind

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:51.941
about some aspects of veterinary science

00:02:51.941 --> 00:02:55.840
but instead, I became a
professor of engineering.

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:58.340
So go figure, I did a lot of--

00:02:59.392 --> 00:03:02.245
- Did you learn how to do math?

00:03:02.245 --> 00:03:04.353
- I did learn how to do that

00:03:04.353 --> 00:03:08.853
and actually, I didn't change
in my ability to do math

00:03:11.246 --> 00:03:16.079
until I was 26-years-old and
I was getting out of the army

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:19.417
and I suddenly realized
that all my friends

00:03:19.417 --> 00:03:23.290
who had a really good technical background

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:27.995
just had more doors open
for them professionally

00:03:27.995 --> 00:03:32.213
and, I mean, I would
never trade the fact that

00:03:32.213 --> 00:03:36.730
I really pursued my dream of
learning the language well

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:40.867
although my Russian is
now very, very rusty

00:03:40.867 --> 00:03:43.799
unless I have a little bit of
wine or something like that

00:03:43.799 --> 00:03:45.835
but we won't go there.

00:03:45.835 --> 00:03:48.585
It comes right back at that point

00:03:50.858 --> 00:03:54.060
but I realized that it
would be a good idea

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:56.853
for me to try to broaden my passions

00:03:56.853 --> 00:03:59.307
instead of follow my passions

00:03:59.307 --> 00:04:02.361
and that maybe I really
could start learning

00:04:02.361 --> 00:04:04.252
in math and science.

00:04:04.252 --> 00:04:09.252
And at that time, they didn't
have Khan Academy available.

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:13.267
It could have been so much easier for me.

00:04:15.522 --> 00:04:19.060
So instead, I had to go to the university,

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:22.310
start with remedial high school algebra

00:04:23.237 --> 00:04:26.952
and slowly begin climbing my way upwards

00:04:26.952 --> 00:04:31.035
but the thing is, if you
just are slow and steady

00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:35.863
and start at the lowest level

00:04:35.863 --> 00:04:38.192
where you actually feel comfortable

00:04:38.192 --> 00:04:41.779
and begin gradually
practicing day after day

00:04:41.779 --> 00:04:44.395
after week, after even years,

00:04:44.395 --> 00:04:47.955
you can achieve far more than you ever...

00:04:47.955 --> 00:04:52.859
I mean, my high school algebra
teacher would have been like,

00:04:52.859 --> 00:04:57.692
"You gotta be kidding me, her,
a professor of engineering?

00:04:59.023 --> 00:05:00.573
You're joking."

00:05:00.573 --> 00:05:03.499
I'm actually a distinguished
professor of engineering

00:05:03.499 --> 00:05:04.408
and I even...

00:05:04.408 --> 00:05:06.846
I'm a fellow in some engineering societies

00:05:06.846 --> 00:05:09.107
and I publish in very good journals

00:05:09.107 --> 00:05:11.218
and, I mean, I'm the real deal,

00:05:11.218 --> 00:05:14.122
I'm not a fake engineering professor

00:05:14.122 --> 00:05:18.789
but the higher I got, the
more I fell in love with math.

00:05:19.918 --> 00:05:22.418
Although I do have to say that

00:05:23.481 --> 00:05:26.926
I'm a very mischievous professor

00:05:26.926 --> 00:05:28.619
because sometimes I will go

00:05:28.619 --> 00:05:31.619
and look at some of Sal's tutorials,

00:05:32.886 --> 00:05:37.497
I will put them together and
then I will go and present them

00:05:37.497 --> 00:05:39.554
with a courtesy thank you to Sal,

00:05:39.554 --> 00:05:41.971
but he just presents things so beautifully

00:05:41.971 --> 00:05:46.971
that sometimes even I was
learning things at advanced levels

00:05:47.406 --> 00:05:49.133
because he can help you

00:05:49.133 --> 00:05:54.114
actually just understand
some of those fundamental

00:05:54.114 --> 00:05:56.525
in actually quite simple concepts,

00:05:56.525 --> 00:05:59.861
at least simple once he
gets done explaining things.

00:05:59.861 --> 00:06:03.861
So, I think that we really
have not as educators

00:06:05.235 --> 00:06:08.568
acknowledged the value of great teaching

00:06:10.249 --> 00:06:14.837
and great explanations in
helping students to move forward

00:06:14.837 --> 00:06:18.990
and be successful at
difficult areas like science,

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:22.475
technology, engineering and math.

00:06:22.475 --> 00:06:23.471
- That totally makes sense

00:06:23.471 --> 00:06:28.113
so, how did you take being
that professor of engineering

00:06:28.113 --> 00:06:31.253
who I'm sure was publishing
about engineering

00:06:31.253 --> 00:06:32.828
and start moving into thinking

00:06:32.828 --> 00:06:35.617
about how to help more people learn

00:06:35.617 --> 00:06:38.301
and thinking about brain science

00:06:38.301 --> 00:06:40.744
and how to bring that into
our understanding of learning?

00:06:40.744 --> 00:06:44.813
How did you make that connection or shift?

00:06:44.813 --> 00:06:47.085
- Well, because my first degree

00:06:47.085 --> 00:06:50.421
was in Slavic languages and literature,

00:06:50.421 --> 00:06:54.088
I have always had a
broader set of interests

00:06:55.602 --> 00:06:59.769
so it's not like, you have
to be in one discipline

00:07:02.048 --> 00:07:04.265
or silo in your life,

00:07:04.265 --> 00:07:06.916
I've been lucky enough to realize that

00:07:06.916 --> 00:07:11.450
sometimes it's okay to
be in the humanity silo,

00:07:11.450 --> 00:07:14.664
sometimes in the social sciences silo

00:07:14.664 --> 00:07:18.247
and sometimes in the
more hard science silo

00:07:19.289 --> 00:07:21.956
and so I've always liked writing

00:07:22.831 --> 00:07:26.155
as well as doing math and engineering

00:07:26.155 --> 00:07:30.822
and I kept that up even when
I was doing my dissertation

00:07:32.911 --> 00:07:35.221
and even as I had my early years

00:07:35.221 --> 00:07:37.204
as a professor of engineering,

00:07:37.204 --> 00:07:39.696
of course I kept it secret

00:07:39.696 --> 00:07:41.812
that I was still writing on the side

00:07:41.812 --> 00:07:45.645
because other engineers
especially in academia

00:07:48.265 --> 00:07:50.994
were not necessarily
on board with the idea

00:07:50.994 --> 00:07:54.340
of being broader than just engineering.

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:57.349
But because I like to write,

00:07:57.349 --> 00:08:01.388
I was always looking at
questions that interested me

00:08:01.388 --> 00:08:04.857
and that led me to a lot of research

00:08:04.857 --> 00:08:08.406
into what is going on
in cognitive psychology

00:08:08.406 --> 00:08:11.583
and a good thing about
looking at a discipline

00:08:11.583 --> 00:08:14.730
when you're trained in a
completely different discipline

00:08:14.730 --> 00:08:17.136
is you can more easily see holes

00:08:17.136 --> 00:08:19.557
and gaps in that discipline.

00:08:19.557 --> 00:08:24.401
So, I saw a lot of great
research in cognitive psychology

00:08:24.401 --> 00:08:27.095
and a lot of glaring holes

00:08:27.095 --> 00:08:30.649
and assumptions that
really couldn't be made

00:08:30.649 --> 00:08:35.024
because there was no solid
scientific foundation.

00:08:35.024 --> 00:08:39.024
And I also worked and
learned as much as I could

00:08:39.905 --> 00:08:43.558
about neuroscience and even genetics,

00:08:43.558 --> 00:08:46.041
so, what do the hard sciences tell us

00:08:46.041 --> 00:08:49.370
about how our brain is working?

00:08:49.370 --> 00:08:54.037
So then, one day, one of my
students asked the question,

00:08:55.097 --> 00:08:55.952
how did...

00:08:55.952 --> 00:08:59.553
He found out about my
terrible past as a math monkey

00:08:59.553 --> 00:09:00.637
and he said,

00:09:00.637 --> 00:09:04.282
"How did you change your
brain to be successful

00:09:04.282 --> 00:09:05.945
in math and science?"

00:09:05.945 --> 00:09:08.848
And so I wrote him a little email

00:09:08.848 --> 00:09:11.235
and then I thought, "I
like to write books,

00:09:11.235 --> 00:09:12.782
why don't I write a book about this?

00:09:12.782 --> 00:09:15.108
I can research it even more."

00:09:15.108 --> 00:09:18.108
And that led to me beginning to work

00:09:19.450 --> 00:09:22.215
in the discipline of education

00:09:22.215 --> 00:09:26.048
and what I found is that
a lot of educators...

00:09:28.917 --> 00:09:30.944
That there's sort of a disconnection,

00:09:30.944 --> 00:09:35.277
it's like, I know how to
teach secondary mathematics

00:09:37.701 --> 00:09:40.354
and I'm really good at
it and that's what I do

00:09:40.354 --> 00:09:44.429
but they don't really look
at the connection of people

00:09:44.429 --> 00:09:46.136
going out into the working world

00:09:46.136 --> 00:09:49.123
with that secondary mathematics education

00:09:49.123 --> 00:09:51.969
and whether it actually fits out there,

00:09:51.969 --> 00:09:54.062
whether it actually builds a good bridge

00:09:54.062 --> 00:09:57.013
for learning in science, engineering

00:09:57.013 --> 00:09:59.164
and technology and mathematics

00:09:59.164 --> 00:10:01.497
and so I found that there's,

00:10:04.029 --> 00:10:08.212
I think an important
need for people like me

00:10:08.212 --> 00:10:11.212
who help educators and help students

00:10:13.035 --> 00:10:15.952
and teachers to see more concretely

00:10:19.492 --> 00:10:23.617
the complex interconnection
between education,

00:10:23.617 --> 00:10:26.534
neuroscience, cognitive psychology,

00:10:27.722 --> 00:10:30.163
what's going on in the practical world

00:10:30.163 --> 00:10:34.392
and tie that together in a
way that people can clearly

00:10:34.392 --> 00:10:36.772
and easily see what's going on.

00:10:36.772 --> 00:10:40.000
And that's sort of how
my work has emerged.

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:43.077
I think people just liked it so much,

00:10:43.077 --> 00:10:45.364
it was very encouraging for me

00:10:45.364 --> 00:10:47.781
and that kinda kept me going.

00:10:49.355 --> 00:10:50.188
- That's great

00:10:50.188 --> 00:10:54.032
and so your new book is,
"Uncommon Sense Teaching."

00:10:54.032 --> 00:10:58.302
So, tell me about that title,
why is it uncommon sense?

00:10:58.302 --> 00:10:59.574
- Oh, okay.

00:10:59.574 --> 00:11:01.241
So, first I thought,

00:11:02.704 --> 00:11:05.703
a lot of these ideas
are just common sense.

00:11:05.703 --> 00:11:10.536
So, my colleague and co-author,
Beth Rogowsky is a K-12...

00:11:11.648 --> 00:11:15.398
She's a teacher of
teachers who teach to K-12

00:11:19.142 --> 00:11:23.562
but she has a lot of experience
herself in K-12 classrooms

00:11:23.562 --> 00:11:27.451
and so, I had my bright idea, I was like,

00:11:27.451 --> 00:11:31.538
"Beth, we should call our
book "Common Sense Teaching,"

00:11:31.538 --> 00:11:33.871
and Beth about blew a gasket

00:11:34.984 --> 00:11:36.366
because she said,

00:11:36.366 --> 00:11:41.275
"A lot of these ideas, you may
think they're common sense,

00:11:41.275 --> 00:11:44.608
but people are really not aware of them.

00:11:46.875 --> 00:11:49.269
Sometimes it's simply not what is taught

00:11:49.269 --> 00:11:53.844
in schools of pedagogy, even
though it is very well grounded

00:11:53.844 --> 00:11:58.177
with profoundly effective
research from neuroscience

00:11:59.192 --> 00:12:01.700
about how we think and learn."

00:12:01.700 --> 00:12:05.402
So, I went away and stood for a while

00:12:05.402 --> 00:12:10.402
because it's like, "Here, my
great idea was all shot down."

00:12:11.006 --> 00:12:13.669
But Beth was quite right about that

00:12:13.669 --> 00:12:16.698
and then finally, one day
it just popped into my head,

00:12:16.698 --> 00:12:20.710
well, what about uncommon sense teaching?

00:12:20.710 --> 00:12:24.156
And Beth wrote me back and she was like,

00:12:24.156 --> 00:12:26.128
"Well, I didn't like the title

00:12:26.128 --> 00:12:28.656
but I asked a bunch of friends
and they really liked it,

00:12:28.656 --> 00:12:30.804
so, now I think I like it too."

00:12:30.804 --> 00:12:33.804
So, that's how the topic came about.

00:12:34.677 --> 00:12:36.633
- Can you give us an example of something

00:12:36.633 --> 00:12:40.858
that seems like common
sense but maybe it's not?

00:12:40.858 --> 00:12:42.441
That commonly used?

00:12:43.426 --> 00:12:46.093
- Well, so, oh, there's so many.

00:12:47.922 --> 00:12:52.005
So, you will often hear
from people who will say,

00:12:52.925 --> 00:12:54.104
you don't need to learn that

00:12:54.104 --> 00:12:56.774
because you can always look it up

00:12:56.774 --> 00:13:00.191
and the reality is, let's say our French,

00:13:03.778 --> 00:13:05.209
I love the sound of French,

00:13:05.209 --> 00:13:06.925
I love French.

00:13:06.925 --> 00:13:11.089
Could I speak French if I just
always went and looked it up

00:13:11.089 --> 00:13:12.901
on Google Translate?

00:13:12.901 --> 00:13:15.811
I mean, I wouldn't have
that neural structure,

00:13:15.811 --> 00:13:20.561
I would have absolutely no
comprehension really of French

00:13:21.415 --> 00:13:24.647
if I just always went and looked it up.

00:13:24.647 --> 00:13:25.789
So, people might think,

00:13:25.789 --> 00:13:29.371
Well, okay, I'll grant
you that for the language

00:13:29.371 --> 00:13:32.760
but it's like this for
almost everything else.

00:13:32.760 --> 00:13:35.370
Well, actually, it isn't.

00:13:35.370 --> 00:13:38.620
You do not become an expert at anything

00:13:41.115 --> 00:13:45.198
unless you have those key
ideas embedded in links

00:13:46.449 --> 00:13:49.754
between neurons of long-term memory.

00:13:49.754 --> 00:13:54.337
And that can only happen
through not only explanations,

00:13:56.598 --> 00:14:01.183
but through your own active
practice with the material.

00:14:01.183 --> 00:14:04.893
Even something as seemingly simple as,

00:14:04.893 --> 00:14:08.997
"Well, I want you to compare
and contrast the French

00:14:08.997 --> 00:14:13.181
and the Russian and the
American Revolutions."

00:14:13.181 --> 00:14:16.731
Well, you could just think about it as,

00:14:16.731 --> 00:14:19.609
"I can always go look it up."

00:14:19.609 --> 00:14:21.290
But if you had to go

00:14:21.290 --> 00:14:24.707
and look up when did each of those occur?

00:14:25.849 --> 00:14:27.539
What were the key aspects?

00:14:27.539 --> 00:14:29.622
Who were the key players?

00:14:30.567 --> 00:14:33.891
All of these things, if
you had to go look that up

00:14:33.891 --> 00:14:36.258
because you didn't bother to put anything

00:14:36.258 --> 00:14:38.752
in your long-term memory,

00:14:38.752 --> 00:14:41.268
you don't really understand

00:14:41.268 --> 00:14:43.554
any of those fundamental concepts

00:14:43.554 --> 00:14:45.671
and we've actually seen this play out

00:14:45.671 --> 00:14:49.921
with things like students
who confuse the civil war

00:14:52.443 --> 00:14:54.337
with civil rights

00:14:54.337 --> 00:14:56.507
and they think that Abraham Lincoln

00:14:56.507 --> 00:14:58.924
maybe knew Martin Luther King

00:14:59.895 --> 00:15:03.422
because they didn't have
to memorize any dates

00:15:03.422 --> 00:15:05.869
that are affiliated with anything.

00:15:05.869 --> 00:15:10.869
So, building really advanced
higher level, Bloom's Taxonomy,

00:15:14.300 --> 00:15:19.217
understanding of information
arises in a very important way

00:15:21.336 --> 00:15:24.919
from our putting of
basic fundamental facts

00:15:27.122 --> 00:15:29.690
into our long-term memory.

00:15:29.690 --> 00:15:33.915
So, I mean, that's just one
example, I could probably give--

00:15:33.915 --> 00:15:35.266
- Yeah, yeah.

00:15:35.266 --> 00:15:37.565
So, I know, that's great.

00:15:37.565 --> 00:15:40.884
So, that's the declarative
knowledge side of things.

00:15:40.884 --> 00:15:44.028
In the book, you also talk
about procedural skills

00:15:44.028 --> 00:15:48.578
and what kinds of things are
we learning from brain science

00:15:48.578 --> 00:15:52.245
about being able to
learn procedural skills?

00:15:54.050 --> 00:15:58.717
- So, the brain has almost
like two major super highways

00:16:01.496 --> 00:16:05.135
that it uses to deposit information

00:16:05.135 --> 00:16:07.166
in your long-term memory.

00:16:07.166 --> 00:16:11.561
The first superhighway is
the declarative highway

00:16:11.561 --> 00:16:15.437
or pathway and that goes
through the hippocampus.

00:16:15.437 --> 00:16:17.678
And we've long known that

00:16:17.678 --> 00:16:22.013
if you like happen to be
missing your hippocampus,

00:16:22.013 --> 00:16:25.717
"Oh, you know, I put it
around here someplace."

00:16:25.717 --> 00:16:27.240
If you're missing it,

00:16:27.240 --> 00:16:29.578
you can't really depositive

00:16:29.578 --> 00:16:31.424
things you've learned declaratively

00:16:31.424 --> 00:16:33.617
into your long-term memory.

00:16:33.617 --> 00:16:37.034
So, a very famous patient, Henry Molaison

00:16:38.067 --> 00:16:40.891
had both of his hippocampi removed

00:16:40.891 --> 00:16:45.096
and if you were introduced
to him, he would greet you

00:16:45.096 --> 00:16:48.729
and he'd be a lovely man
and you walk out of the room

00:16:48.729 --> 00:16:51.279
and then you wait a minute
or two, you walk back in

00:16:51.279 --> 00:16:53.569
and he would not remember you

00:16:53.569 --> 00:16:56.903
and you'd have to be reintroduced.

00:16:56.903 --> 00:17:00.736
But let's say that you
walked in and met Henry

00:17:01.881 --> 00:17:04.469
and you were a mischievous sort

00:17:04.469 --> 00:17:07.568
and you had in your hand a pin.

00:17:07.568 --> 00:17:09.953
I'm not suggesting to do this in real life

00:17:09.953 --> 00:17:12.558
but actually, psychologists
have done this with patients

00:17:12.558 --> 00:17:15.236
who are similar to Henry.

00:17:15.236 --> 00:17:17.486
And you shook Henry's hand,

00:17:19.261 --> 00:17:22.843
what would happen is, if you walked out

00:17:22.843 --> 00:17:27.422
and you walk back in and you
introduced yourself again,

00:17:27.422 --> 00:17:29.460
Henry would not know who you were

00:17:29.460 --> 00:17:31.183
and he'd be very glad to meet you

00:17:31.183 --> 00:17:32.977
and if you reached out with your hand

00:17:32.977 --> 00:17:37.477
to shake hands with him, he
would not shake your hand.

00:17:38.515 --> 00:17:42.659
He had learned and he
had remembered something.

00:17:42.659 --> 00:17:46.463
And that was because
he was able to remember

00:17:46.463 --> 00:17:50.716
about the pinprick through
his procedural system

00:17:50.716 --> 00:17:53.633
of the basal ganglia, that deposit,

00:17:55.249 --> 00:17:56.943
sets of links in long-term memory

00:17:56.943 --> 00:18:01.391
and that's why Henry could
not remember you or your face

00:18:01.391 --> 00:18:04.454
but kinda remember that you are associated

00:18:04.454 --> 00:18:07.844
with a handshake that is a big aoi

00:18:07.844 --> 00:18:11.273
and so this procedural kind of learning

00:18:11.273 --> 00:18:14.523
is actually like half of what we learn,

00:18:15.823 --> 00:18:18.690
anything that we have to know really well

00:18:18.690 --> 00:18:21.564
so that we don't really
wanna have to think about it,

00:18:21.564 --> 00:18:24.384
like our native language for example,

00:18:24.384 --> 00:18:26.551
we learn that procedurally

00:18:28.505 --> 00:18:32.461
and that means that we
can speak extemporaneously

00:18:32.461 --> 00:18:37.461
without having to think about
what we're actually saying.

00:18:37.812 --> 00:18:40.145
And this is why for example,

00:18:41.218 --> 00:18:44.840
let's say that you have
a simultaneous translator

00:18:44.840 --> 00:18:48.923
or a translator who has
learned another language,

00:18:49.804 --> 00:18:52.567
but it's not their native language.

00:18:52.567 --> 00:18:56.067
It can actually be exhausting to translate

00:18:57.687 --> 00:19:01.854
because you're often using
that declarative system

00:19:03.653 --> 00:19:05.461
to think about things

00:19:05.461 --> 00:19:07.697
whereas if you can just think about it

00:19:07.697 --> 00:19:09.501
using your procedural system,

00:19:09.501 --> 00:19:11.552
speaking in your native language,

00:19:11.552 --> 00:19:14.424
it's not exhausting at
all because it's habitual

00:19:14.424 --> 00:19:19.295
and you've got those sets of
links in procedural memory.

00:19:19.295 --> 00:19:21.902
So, what this means is,

00:19:21.902 --> 00:19:25.319
for learners, it is invaluable to develop

00:19:27.876 --> 00:19:32.876
not only declarative kind of
factually related sets of links

00:19:32.899 --> 00:19:36.850
in long-term memory, oh,
the date is this date

00:19:36.850 --> 00:19:40.255
but also procedural sets of links.

00:19:40.255 --> 00:19:44.172
So, this relates more to
like complex patterns.

00:19:47.746 --> 00:19:51.719
How do you work math problems like this

00:19:51.719 --> 00:19:56.719
as opposed to like another, as
opposed to like a third type?

00:19:57.654 --> 00:20:00.109
How do you work all of
these kinds of problems?

00:20:00.109 --> 00:20:02.609
You only develop the intuition

00:20:04.510 --> 00:20:09.455
or seeming intuition of how
to do these kinds of problems

00:20:09.455 --> 00:20:12.819
by practicing with lots
of different types,

00:20:12.819 --> 00:20:14.281
getting lots of feedback

00:20:14.281 --> 00:20:16.147
about whether or what you've done

00:20:16.147 --> 00:20:19.058
to try to solve that problem is correct

00:20:19.058 --> 00:20:21.231
and then trying again

00:20:21.231 --> 00:20:25.564
and interleaving different,
closely related examples

00:20:27.522 --> 00:20:30.506
so that you can see for example,

00:20:30.506 --> 00:20:33.339
what is the geometric distribution

00:20:34.634 --> 00:20:38.467
versus what is the negative
binomial distribution

00:20:38.467 --> 00:20:41.623
versus what is the binomial distribution

00:20:41.623 --> 00:20:45.240
and you can actually see when you use one,

00:20:45.240 --> 00:20:48.635
when you use another and
how to do these things.

00:20:48.635 --> 00:20:52.689
Oftentimes in classes,
what you actually learn is,

00:20:52.689 --> 00:20:57.639
"Okay, here's an example
of binomial distribution."

00:20:57.639 --> 00:21:00.967
And you do like a dozen problems of it

00:21:00.967 --> 00:21:03.610
and you think you know it really well

00:21:03.610 --> 00:21:06.238
but then you're faced with a new problem

00:21:06.238 --> 00:21:09.864
that's negative binomial and you're like,

00:21:09.864 --> 00:21:14.085
"No, wait a minute, what's going on here?"

00:21:14.085 --> 00:21:16.147
If you haven't practiced
to train the difference

00:21:16.147 --> 00:21:18.057
between these things,

00:21:18.057 --> 00:21:22.645
then you don't know what
those differences are

00:21:22.645 --> 00:21:25.017
and this is where a lot of practice

00:21:25.017 --> 00:21:29.121
with your procedural learning
system can be so valuable

00:21:29.121 --> 00:21:30.954
and I have to ask you,

00:21:33.041 --> 00:21:35.833
what does Khan Academy do

00:21:35.833 --> 00:21:39.833
to help learners with
their procedural learning?

00:21:41.820 --> 00:21:44.668
- Well, what you were just describing,

00:21:44.668 --> 00:21:48.533
hopefully those out listening
have heard a lot of things

00:21:48.533 --> 00:21:50.998
that sound like Khan Academy

00:21:50.998 --> 00:21:53.865
because we are absolutely based in...

00:21:53.865 --> 00:21:57.180
Well, some folks know us as videos,

00:21:57.180 --> 00:21:59.763
the exercises that are woven in

00:22:01.219 --> 00:22:03.140
and linked to those videos

00:22:03.140 --> 00:22:06.736
are key to the learning
experience at Khan Academy

00:22:06.736 --> 00:22:11.505
and so as we see those exercises
as the active learning,

00:22:11.505 --> 00:22:14.282
that practice component
you were talking about

00:22:14.282 --> 00:22:17.339
where students can start
doing a number of problems

00:22:17.339 --> 00:22:19.452
that are kind of similar to each other

00:22:19.452 --> 00:22:22.075
and then somewhat different

00:22:22.075 --> 00:22:27.075
and then, when they get to
having done a couple few of those

00:22:27.092 --> 00:22:29.609
in a row, we actually give them problems

00:22:29.609 --> 00:22:31.654
that have a couple of
different related skills

00:22:31.654 --> 00:22:34.224
and they have to figure
out which one to use,

00:22:34.224 --> 00:22:35.724
which one to apply

00:22:36.686 --> 00:22:40.309
as kind of the extra
added layer of difficulty.

00:22:40.309 --> 00:22:42.174
But essentially, that is the key thing

00:22:42.174 --> 00:22:45.651
that we at Khan Academy see
as one of our main purposes

00:22:45.651 --> 00:22:50.043
as a supplemental tool
for students in classrooms

00:22:50.043 --> 00:22:51.171
and working on their own

00:22:51.171 --> 00:22:53.695
to be able to provide that extra practice

00:22:53.695 --> 00:22:56.568
so those links you were talking about,

00:22:56.568 --> 00:23:00.222
those neural connections are
forming and getting stronger.

00:23:00.222 --> 00:23:02.944
- Well, you really could
not be more in line

00:23:02.944 --> 00:23:05.374
with what brain science is revealing

00:23:05.374 --> 00:23:07.551
about how we should learn.

00:23:07.551 --> 00:23:08.664
It does make me laugh

00:23:08.664 --> 00:23:12.664
because notice how you
framed what you're doing,

00:23:14.205 --> 00:23:19.205
it's like closely related
concepts that you're interleaving.

00:23:19.680 --> 00:23:23.597
Sometimes teachers can
unwittingly get the idea

00:23:25.175 --> 00:23:28.417
that interleaving means
just having something

00:23:28.417 --> 00:23:32.241
and then having something
different but it isn't.

00:23:32.241 --> 00:23:35.343
The key to interleaving
is it has to have...

00:23:35.343 --> 00:23:39.535
You want students to notice the difference

00:23:39.535 --> 00:23:42.285
between closely related patterns.

00:23:43.382 --> 00:23:46.715
So, like, if you're teaching punctuation

00:23:47.655 --> 00:23:49.168
and you interleave that

00:23:49.168 --> 00:23:52.751
with things like
questions about metaphors,

00:23:55.568 --> 00:23:58.259
that's not interleaving,
they're too far apart.

00:23:58.259 --> 00:24:00.557
But if you talk about what's a metaphor

00:24:00.557 --> 00:24:04.709
as opposed to a simile,
as opposed to an analogy,

00:24:04.709 --> 00:24:07.292
now you're talking interleaving

00:24:09.627 --> 00:24:11.574
so that's when you really start building

00:24:11.574 --> 00:24:14.530
that procedural intuitions

00:24:14.530 --> 00:24:18.447
with the closely related,
interleaved concepts.

00:24:20.109 --> 00:24:23.415
- Great, so, as usual time is flying.

00:24:23.415 --> 00:24:26.023
I wanna get to a couple of
questions that are coming in

00:24:26.023 --> 00:24:28.093
from the folks that are watching.

00:24:28.093 --> 00:24:29.534
I know.

00:24:29.534 --> 00:24:33.801
So, it's funny you just talked
about similes and metaphors.

00:24:33.801 --> 00:24:36.982
This is kind of a different
question but relates to it.

00:24:36.982 --> 00:24:40.703
So, Susanna Garcia
Domingos from YouTube asks,

00:24:40.703 --> 00:24:41.658
"Hello, Dr. Oakley,

00:24:41.658 --> 00:24:44.818
could you please share
about the use of metaphors

00:24:44.818 --> 00:24:47.379
and analogies to help people learn?

00:24:47.379 --> 00:24:50.516
How could we effectively use them?"

00:24:50.516 --> 00:24:53.433
- Oh, Gemini, can I share something

00:24:55.332 --> 00:25:00.072
that would show this idea
very, very clearly if I can?

00:25:00.072 --> 00:25:02.471
Come on Bob, tell me you can...

00:25:02.471 --> 00:25:03.968
Or is it possible?

00:25:03.968 --> 00:25:05.059
If it isn't--

00:25:05.059 --> 00:25:08.997
- I don't think we can do a screen share.

00:25:08.997 --> 00:25:10.784
- If you wanna point someone to somewhere

00:25:10.784 --> 00:25:12.554
where they could look that bit

00:25:12.554 --> 00:25:14.137
or hang on whatever

00:25:17.097 --> 00:25:17.989
- So, here's the thing.

00:25:17.989 --> 00:25:21.822
So, you create this set
of interlinked neurons

00:25:24.862 --> 00:25:26.960
and you can just think of it like

00:25:26.960 --> 00:25:30.877
you've got this nice,
little, golden necklace

00:25:30.877 --> 00:25:33.294
with nice links between them.

00:25:34.944 --> 00:25:39.713
So, let's say you know
what the flow of water is.

00:25:39.713 --> 00:25:42.431
That actually in your own mind,

00:25:42.431 --> 00:25:45.014
that is a set of linked neurons

00:25:48.218 --> 00:25:50.657
and it's sort of like a complex set

00:25:50.657 --> 00:25:53.107
so you've got this net of neurons

00:25:53.107 --> 00:25:57.774
and that net is your understanding
that you've developed

00:25:58.886 --> 00:26:03.335
ever since you were a little
kid watching water flow.

00:26:03.335 --> 00:26:06.331
Now let's say that
you're trying to explain

00:26:06.331 --> 00:26:08.824
how electrical current flows.

00:26:08.824 --> 00:26:12.657
So that's like electrons
moving the other way,

00:26:14.952 --> 00:26:17.220
then you actually think of electron...

00:26:17.220 --> 00:26:20.605
But anyway, so it's
the motion of particles

00:26:20.605 --> 00:26:24.688
which is somewhat analogous
to the flow of water.

00:26:25.884 --> 00:26:30.182
Because you already have that set of links

00:26:30.182 --> 00:26:33.373
for understanding how water flows,

00:26:33.373 --> 00:26:35.742
you can much more easily understand

00:26:35.742 --> 00:26:38.439
the flow of electrical current

00:26:38.439 --> 00:26:42.077
because it's got a lot of
the same, those key ideas

00:26:42.077 --> 00:26:44.160
of movement of particles.

00:26:46.110 --> 00:26:49.193
So, it turns out that using metaphors

00:26:51.252 --> 00:26:55.060
or using analogies are a similarity,

00:26:55.060 --> 00:26:58.630
a likeness between something you know

00:26:58.630 --> 00:27:01.411
and something you wish to convey

00:27:01.411 --> 00:27:06.411
is an incredibly powerful tool
in education and in learning.

00:27:08.117 --> 00:27:11.919
And it's in part related to a theory

00:27:11.919 --> 00:27:14.169
called neural reuse theory

00:27:14.169 --> 00:27:16.938
which means if we've already built

00:27:16.938 --> 00:27:18.309
our understanding of something,

00:27:18.309 --> 00:27:23.309
we can reuse some of those
same neurons to help us better

00:27:23.736 --> 00:27:28.196
and more easily understand
new ideas and new concepts.

00:27:28.196 --> 00:27:31.196
So, sometimes people will say, "Yes"

00:27:33.218 --> 00:27:35.768
but the analogy breaks down after a while,

00:27:35.768 --> 00:27:37.288
the metaphor breaks down.

00:27:37.288 --> 00:27:39.883
If you get to a quantum level

00:27:39.883 --> 00:27:42.673
in how electrical current flows,

00:27:42.673 --> 00:27:46.181
it's not at all like how
water flows and that's true.

00:27:46.181 --> 00:27:48.523
But whenever a metaphor breaks down,

00:27:48.523 --> 00:27:50.568
you just get a new metaphor.

00:27:50.568 --> 00:27:52.735
And indeed, Emanuel Derman

00:27:54.678 --> 00:27:59.511
who's a great physicist turned
financial analyst said that

00:28:01.373 --> 00:28:05.373
all mathematical equations
are simply metaphors.

00:28:07.510 --> 00:28:10.959
And I think there's a great
deal of truth to that.

00:28:10.959 --> 00:28:15.402
So anyway, I'm a keen
proponent of metaphors

00:28:15.402 --> 00:28:17.963
and especially visual metaphors

00:28:17.963 --> 00:28:22.667
because they can help students
learn so much more quickly.

00:28:22.667 --> 00:28:24.913
- Great, thank you.

00:28:24.913 --> 00:28:25.746
Let's have...

00:28:25.746 --> 00:28:28.436
I think we have time for one more

00:28:28.436 --> 00:28:31.540
from Akshat Doobie on YouTube.

00:28:31.540 --> 00:28:36.490
"I've been struggling with
making habits for a long time.

00:28:36.490 --> 00:28:41.124
What are some tips to help
me create a new habit?"

00:28:41.124 --> 00:28:45.723
- Just do it over and over and
over and over and over again.

00:28:45.723 --> 00:28:48.511
I mean, I know that sounds silly

00:28:48.511 --> 00:28:51.511
but let me just give you an example.

00:28:52.660 --> 00:28:57.410
Let's say that you are driving
home, you're driving home,

00:29:00.269 --> 00:29:04.006
you've done it a hundred times before

00:29:04.006 --> 00:29:07.510
and you think of something else that day

00:29:07.510 --> 00:29:09.287
that is really important

00:29:09.287 --> 00:29:13.933
like you should have put
something into a report

00:29:13.933 --> 00:29:15.377
and you forgot to do it.

00:29:15.377 --> 00:29:18.849
Well, 45 minutes later, you arrive at home

00:29:18.849 --> 00:29:22.432
and you can't even
remember how you got home.

00:29:22.432 --> 00:29:24.599
Well, how did you get home then?

00:29:24.599 --> 00:29:26.873
I mean, how do you learn to do that?

00:29:26.873 --> 00:29:30.110
Well, you have driven home so many times

00:29:30.110 --> 00:29:34.267
that actually your procedural
system has learned it

00:29:34.267 --> 00:29:35.336
and you can drive home

00:29:35.336 --> 00:29:37.953
without even thinking
about it as a consequence

00:29:37.953 --> 00:29:41.040
unless somebody does
something bizarre in a car

00:29:41.040 --> 00:29:44.163
right ahead of you or something.

00:29:44.163 --> 00:29:48.830
So, what this means is, in
our daily lives, for example,

00:29:50.334 --> 00:29:53.167
let's see, I wanted our daughters,

00:29:54.418 --> 00:29:59.394
our two daughters to have all
career doors open for them.

00:29:59.394 --> 00:30:03.644
So, for many, many children,
mathematics is not fun

00:30:05.987 --> 00:30:09.440
and when you try to make
it fun, it may become fun

00:30:09.440 --> 00:30:13.742
but it may not be necessarily
a good learning experience.

00:30:13.742 --> 00:30:17.742
So, I just made a habit
of 20 minutes every day,

00:30:20.224 --> 00:30:24.271
our girls did some extra math practice.

00:30:24.271 --> 00:30:25.735
We used Kumon Math,

00:30:25.735 --> 00:30:28.984
I think if Khan Academy had
been around at that time,

00:30:28.984 --> 00:30:31.122
we would have been using Khan Academy

00:30:31.122 --> 00:30:33.362
but they did this extra practice

00:30:33.362 --> 00:30:37.076
interleaving closely related concepts

00:30:37.076 --> 00:30:40.259
and they didn't wanna
do it most of the time,

00:30:40.259 --> 00:30:42.513
it was sometimes pushing and so forth

00:30:42.513 --> 00:30:46.537
but it was the habit
that I developed in them,

00:30:46.537 --> 00:30:50.963
you just do this for 20 minutes
a day, that's who you are.

00:30:50.963 --> 00:30:55.130
And, Byrgaly, our older
daughter who's, let's say,

00:30:56.260 --> 00:30:59.343
not a natural talent with mathematics

00:31:00.935 --> 00:31:05.160
finished her medical residency at Stanford

00:31:05.160 --> 00:31:07.457
and it's this early training,

00:31:07.457 --> 00:31:10.100
that just sort of a little
bit of extra practice,

00:31:10.100 --> 00:31:14.506
20 minutes a day, that
habit actually helped her

00:31:14.506 --> 00:31:18.197
so she did have all
career doors open for her

00:31:18.197 --> 00:31:20.668
even if you might have said early on,

00:31:20.668 --> 00:31:25.559
"This kid's, she's not gonna
go anywhere with math."

00:31:25.559 --> 00:31:28.097
Our younger daughter was
more natural at math,

00:31:28.097 --> 00:31:32.014
she simply hated it but
she had to do it anyway

00:31:33.512 --> 00:31:36.891
and she went off and she became an artist

00:31:36.891 --> 00:31:39.106
and now she's back at school

00:31:39.106 --> 00:31:42.088
getting her master's degree in statistics.

00:31:42.088 --> 00:31:46.292
So, it did keep those doors open for all.

00:31:46.292 --> 00:31:49.674
So, for me, Duolingo, I use Duolingo

00:31:49.674 --> 00:31:54.177
and I practice my Spanish
or my Russian each day

00:31:54.177 --> 00:31:55.760
and it helps a lot.

00:31:57.590 --> 00:32:02.007
- Great, so, I think that's
a good theme to close on,

00:32:04.045 --> 00:32:07.755
that Keep practicing, it
builds those pathways,

00:32:07.755 --> 00:32:10.047
it builds those skills.

00:32:10.047 --> 00:32:12.230
Thank you for joining us today.

00:32:12.230 --> 00:32:16.839
Again, the new book, publication
day today, congratulations,

00:32:16.839 --> 00:32:18.850
"Uncommon Sense Teaching."

00:32:18.850 --> 00:32:22.493
For those of you that are
looking for some new guidance

00:32:22.493 --> 00:32:24.674
in the classroom, I highly recommend it.

00:32:24.674 --> 00:32:26.488
Definitely pick it up and get some,

00:32:26.488 --> 00:32:28.671
both the suggestions

